1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:03,478
The brush clears a small clay lamp.

2
00:00:03,478 --> 00:00:10,394
Jain tradition remembers the lamps lit at the time of Mahavira's liberation, and the festival is still marked with lights.

3
00:00:10,394 --> 00:00:17,286
This faith did not rest on a creator god, but on conduct. Why would a lamp be central to it?

4
00:00:33,717 --> 00:00:39,448
How do we know? Mostly from Jain texts, called Agamas, written in Prakrit.

5
00:00:39,448 --> 00:00:48,296
Archaeologists dug up Jain caves and inscriptions of later centuries. Our dates for Mahavira come from tradition, and they differ.

6
00:01:03,217 --> 00:01:10,419
Jain tradition speaks of twenty-four Tirthankaras, teachers who show the way across. The first was Rishabhanatha.

7
00:01:10,419 --> 00:01:14,978
The twenty-third was Parshvanatha. The twenty-fourth was Mahavira.

8
00:01:14,978 --> 00:01:22,284
Mahavira was born as Vardhamana, a prince of a clan near Vaishali in Bihar, about the sixth century BCE.

9
00:01:22,284 --> 00:01:31,429
Jain tradition gives 599 BCE, and many books give about 540 BCE. At thirty, he left his home.

10
00:01:31,429 --> 00:01:35,219
After years of hard discipline, he gained perfect knowledge.

11
00:01:35,219 --> 00:01:39,777
He was called Jina, the conqueror, and his followers were called Jains.

12
00:01:39,777 --> 00:01:45,526
His teaching rests on three jewels: right faith, right knowledge and right conduct.

13
00:01:45,526 --> 00:01:55,386
He taught five vows: do not harm any living being, which is ahimsa, speak truth, do not steal, do not own more than you need, and keep chastity.

14
00:01:55,386 --> 00:02:02,293
Jainism does not accept a creator god. It teaches karma and rebirth, and the path to liberation.

15
00:02:02,293 --> 00:02:09,417
Later, the community divided into the Digambara, the sky-clad, and the Shvetambara, the white-clad.

16
00:02:09,417 --> 00:02:14,161
Jain texts were written in Prakrit, so common people could follow.

17
00:02:29,083 --> 00:02:36,258
Old name, new name. Early Jain monks were called Nirgranthas, which means those free from bonds.

18
00:02:36,258 --> 00:02:40,791
Later, the followers of the Jinas came to be called Jains.

19
00:02:55,717 --> 00:03:04,950
Carve this tip into memory. Three jewels, five vows. twenty-four Tirthankaras. Three, five and twenty-four.

20
00:03:04,950 --> 00:03:08,397
Say those numbers and the Jain outline follows.

21
00:03:23,317 --> 00:03:39,830
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. How many Tirthankaras are in the Jain tradition? The answer: twenty-four. Number two.

22
00:03:39,830 --> 00:03:50,588
Which vow means not harming any living being? The answer: ahimsa. Number three.

23
00:03:50,588 --> 00:04:02,286
In which language were the early Jain texts written? The answer: Prakrit. Now one line for Mains.

24
00:04:02,286 --> 00:04:06,225
Examine the main teachings of Jainism and its spread.

25
00:04:25,150 --> 00:04:32,754
The lamp goes into its case. It is a small light for a faith of quiet discipline. Three facts to take away.

26
00:04:32,754 --> 00:04:42,956
A lamp, a symbol of knowledge. Three jewels and five vows. Teaching spread in Prakrit. Next gallery: The Buddha's Path.

